Ladybird’s removeCSS() takes away only a sheet that the same extension
added with the same file or code and origin, never a content script’s.
Chromium keys its sheets the same way, with its ScriptExecutor::
 GenerateInjectionKey() (the extension and the file’s URL, or the
code), and WebKit passes each sheet’s style level (author or user)
through its insertCSS() and removeCSS(). A USER sheet joins the
document’s user style sheet, so its !important rules beat the page’s,
as in Chromium and WebKit.

In manifest version 2, tabs.executeScript() follows Chromium’s
ExecuteCodeFunction: A frameId with allFrames picks that frame’s
subtree, an error in that root frame rejects the call, and “No source
code or file specified.” and “Code and file should not be specified at
the same time in the second argument.” keep Chromium’s wording. Gecko’s
ext-tabs-base.js rejects frameId with allFrames as “mutually exclusive”
instead; Ladybird takes Chromium’s reading, which Dark Reader’s Firefox
build doesn’t depend on either way.
